An array is a linear data structure used to store multiple elements of the same data type under a single variable name.
The elements of an array are stored in contiguous memory locations, which allows fast and direct access using index values that start from 0 and go up to n−1.
Arrays make it easy to manage large amounts of data and are commonly processed using loops.
They support operations such as traversal, searching, and sorting, and are widely used in problem-solving and competitive programming. 
However, arrays have a fixed size, which cannot be changed after creation, and insertion or deletion of elements can be time-consuming.
Despite these limitations, arrays are simple, efficient, and form the foundation of many advanced data structures and algorithms.
